#include <iostream>

int gcd(int a, int b) {
    if (a == b) {
        return a;
    }

    if (a == 0) {
        return b;
    }

    if (b == 0) {
        return a;
    }

    if ((~a & 1) && (~b & 1)) {
        return gcd(a >> 1, b >> 1) << 1;
    }

    if ((~a & 1) && (b & 1)) {
        return gcd(a >> 1, b);
    }

    if ((a & 1) && (~b & 1)) {
        return gcd(a, b >> 1);
    }

    if (a > b) {
        return gcd((a - b) >> 1, b);
    }

    return gcd(a, (b - a) >> 1);
}

int main() {
    int a, b;
    std::cout << "Enter two integers: ";
    std::cin >> a >> b;
    int result = gcd(a, b);
    std::cout << "GCD: " << result << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

Explanation:

The code implements the binary GCD algorithm using bitwise operations. The algorithm calculates the GCD of two integers by repeatedly dividing the larger number by 2 until it becomes odd and then dividing the difference between the two numbers by 2 until one of them becomes zero.

The algorithm uses bitwise AND (&), OR (|), NOT (~), left shift (<<), and right shift (>>). These operations allow us to perform arithmetic operations on binary representations of numbers.

The code takes two integers as input and calls the `gcd()` function to calculate their GCD. It then prints the result.

Note that this code assumes that the input integers are positive. If negative numbers are allowed as inputs, the code should be modified accordingly to handle the sign of the numbers.

